We evaluate $\displaystyle \oint_{\gamma} \frac{z}{\sin z}\,dz$, where $\gamma$ is the circle $|z|=2$ traversed **clockwise**.

**Singularities.** The integrand $f(z)=\dfrac{z}{\sin z}$ is singular where $\sin z = 0$, i.e. at $z = n\pi$, $n\in\mathbb{Z}$. Inside the disk $|z|<2$ we need $|n\pi|<2$, i.e. $|n|<2/\pi\approx 0.637$. The only integer satisfying this is $n=0$, so the only candidate singularity inside $\gamma$ is $z=0$.

**Nature of the singularity at $z=0$.** Using the Taylor expansion $\sin z = z - \frac{z^{3}}{6}+\cdots = z\bigl(1-\frac{z^{2}}{6}+\cdots\bigr)$,

$$
\frac{z}{\sin z}=\frac{z}{z\bigl(1-\frac{z^{2}}{6}+\cdots\bigr)}=\frac{1}{1-\frac{z^{2}}{6}+\cdots}=1+\frac{z^{2}}{6}+\cdots .
$$

This is a power series with no $z^{-1}$ term, so $z=0$ is a **removable singularity** and

$$
\operatorname{Res}_{z=0}\frac{z}{\sin z}=0.
$$

**Applying the residue theorem.** Since $\gamma$ is oriented **clockwise** (negative orientation), the residue theorem gives

$$
\oint_{\gamma}\frac{z}{\sin z}\,dz = -2\pi i \sum_{|a|<2}\operatorname{Res}_{z=a}\frac{z}{\sin z} = -2\pi i \cdot 0 = 0.
$$

(Equivalently, $z/\sin z$ extends to a function holomorphic on the entire disk $|z|\le 2$, so by Cauchy's theorem the integral over any closed curve in this region is $0$.)

$$
\boxed{\displaystyle \oint_{\gamma}\frac{z}{\sin z}\,dz = 0.}
$$

